                <p class="font-bold">
                  Excursion 3 – Cycling Through the Alpujarras: Between the
                  Southern Slopes of the Sierra Nevada and the Mediterranean Sea
                </p>
                <p class="">
                  Today we explore one of the most authentic and captivating
                  regions of Andalusia: Las Alpujarras, a land of rich history,
                  rugged natural beauty, and distinctive architecture. This
                  excursion takes us along the southern slopes of the Sierra
                  Nevada, through landscapes that have remained largely
                  unchanged for centuries.
                </p>
                <p class="">
                  The day begins with a transfer to Puerto de la Ragua, a
                  mountain pass situated at an altitude of 2,000 metres on the
                  border between the provinces of Granada and Almería. From
                  here, we embark on a spectacular mostly downhill ride,
                  enjoying breathtaking views over deep valleys, terraced
                  farmland, and, on clear days, even glimpses of the
                  Mediterranean Sea and the distant outline of Africa on the
                  horizon.
                </p>
                <p class="">
                  Las Alpujarras is a region where time seems to stand still.
                  Following the Reconquista, it became the last refuge of the
                  Moriscos, the Muslim inhabitants of Spain. Their legacy
                  remains visible today in the harmonious relationship between
                  people and nature, reflected in the ancient irrigation
                  channels, agricultural terraces, and traditional architecture
                  that characterise the area.
                </p>
                <p class="">
                  Along the route, we pass through several picturesque villages
                  of Berber influence, whose appearance evokes the landscapes of
                  North Africa. Clinging to the mountainsides, these villages
                  are composed of whitewashed cubic houses with flat roofs and
                  distinctive chimney pots, connected by narrow, winding streets
                  that invite exploration and offer a glimpse into a unique
                  cultural heritage.
                </p>
              </div>

